[Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  good evening everyone and welcome to the  beautiful Beverly Wilshire ballroom and  the Academy's 86th scientific and  technical awards  the first time these awards became a  standalone dinner presentation was on  March 29 1978 in the Grand Ballroom of  the Beverly Hilton Kirk Douglas and  Gregory Peck hosted but the side tech  awards go back to the earliest days of  the Academy when they were first  presented as part of the regular awards  dinner at the Biltmore Hotel on November  10 1931 Oscar statuettes were given to  RCA photophone and to RKO studios for  noise reduction recording imagine pre  Dolby and to DuPont film and to the  Eastman Kodak Company for the  manufacture of super-sensitive  panchromatic films these awards have  always had their own ambience separate  from that other red carpet show over on  Hollywood Boulevard  for example the buzz this evening will  not be who is wearing what designer  label but will address far more  consequential matters such as do most  filmmakers really need the tank sized 73  foot Hydra scope self-leveling  telescoping camera crane  or can we squeak by with merely the 32  foot model or even more momentous should  we in hindsight have had a bake-off  between DreamWorks and Pixar Animation  Studios over the Primeau or the presto  character animation systems I'm hoping  that committee chair ray Feeny along  with our resident arbiter that most  articulate spokesman of many things  techno mr. Jonathan Arlen will enlighten  us as he embraces the Oscar statuette  he'll receive this evening  there's no one here who has done more  for our community with his ongoing  analysis of image creation the movies  may be a visual medium but Jonathan is  one of its best wordsmiths  I've been so looking forward to this  evening as awards to tell you my fellow  filmmakers here tonight just how crucial  your work is to the so intricate warp  and woof of today's motion pictures at  every phase of production and post the  amazing tools of Science and Technology  that you continue to develop carry us  ever outward into new and alternative  visions into new worlds and into the  mysteries of our own psyches just where  would our great actors and writers our  directors and producers be without the  amazingly complex hardware and software  creations that you set out before us  this is your night nobody's going to  play you off into a commercial break  the Academy its officers and Board of  Governors are here to recognize and  honor the amazing work you do even  though most of us sitting here have no  idea how to describe it one person who  can step up to this high-tech assignment  is our eloquent host for this evening a  near thirty year veteran of the Royal  Shakespeare Company captain of the  Starship Enterprise  [Music]  hello Academy mer and proud Olympic  torch bearer Patrick Stewart  sir Patrick thank you for being with us  here tonight I want to recognize also  the Academy governors our CEO Don Hudson  as well as past and present members and  project contributors of the SciTech  Council and its director Andy malts this  year  this year we have seen a wider adoption  of the academy color encoding system  aces now for large and for small budget  movies several of which have been  nominated for Academy Awards also the  Society of motion picture and television  engineers simply published this year to  standards based on aces to help with the  long term preservation of our digital  movies a step closer to solving the  dreaded digital dilemma some of us  however might be presumptuous enough to  ask while waiting for this elusive  digital solution there is already a  reliable analog preservation medium  maybe you've heard about it it's called  film  thanks to the scientific and technical  awards committee in its chair ray Feeny  who has the help of volunteers have  committed countless hours to evaluating  and recommending recipients of tonight's  awards when the Academy Museum of Motion  Pictures opens to the world next year be  assured that the story of motion picture  Science and Technology will be every bit  as crucial to its presentation to the  story of our movies as our Dorothy's red  shoes the rosebud sled and 2001's lunar  lander lander and now it's my honor to  introduce the chair of the scientific  and technical Awards Committee for time  scientific and technical Award winner  and Gordon E Sawyer Oscar recipient mr.  Ray Feeny  [Music]  [Applause]

